ction word is unique; (5) evalWord/rootTriple style generation and reduction are inverse on normal forms. If full uniqueness of inverse branch is too strong globally, prove it on a canonical orientation subclass and obtain a deterministic normal-form theorem there. Algorithmically, package the iteration as a terminating reduction procedure and prove soundness/completeness for membership in the Berggren orbit. Cryptographically, express the preserved invariant through lorentzForm or lorentzQ and prove reduction preserves the Pythagorean constraint while separating non-equivalent reduced states. This yields a falsifiable program: either strict descent and uniqueness can be established from existing Berggren matrices, or the conjectured parent rule fails on explicit counterexamples.\n**Concept description**: The key insight is that the Berggren tree on primitive Pythagorean triples already carries a discrete reduction dynamics: a height or norm on triples should strictly decrease under a suitable parent/descent map, turning the geometric generation structure into an algorithmic lattice-reduction procedure with provable termination and rigidity. Why now: the catalog already contains the exact ingredients in mature form across domains \u2014 vetted geometric Berggren steps in FINAL/Geometry/BerggrenRamanujan.lean, cryptographic Lorentz-form encodings in Cryptography/BerggrenLatticeCryptography.lean and Cryptography/BerggrenDiophantineLattice.lean, and an existing bridge file Bridges/AlgebraPythagoreanCryptography/BerggrenLatticeReductionDuality.lean with PrimTriple infrastructure. The proposed direction is to prove a concrete descent theorem: every non-root primitive triple admits a canonical predecessor detected by a monotone Lorentz/Euclidean potential, and repeated predecessor iteration terminates at the root triple. From this, formalize an explicit reduction algorithm on Berggren words/triples, prove correctness and uniqueness of normal forms, and derive cryptographic invariants showing that evaluation words with the same reduced normal form encode the same primitive triple while distinct reduced forms remain separated by the Lorentz invariant. This is a genuine bridge between Geometry, Pythagorean arithmetic, and Cryptography because theorems about Berggren generation become an algorithmic normalization pipeline for structured public-key data rather than just an existence tree.\n**Novelty estimate**: 0.9\n**Breakthrough potential**: 0.86\nResearch domain: Bridges\nResearch mode: prove\n\n\n### Lean 4 Sketch\nLikely build in Bridges/AlgebraPythagoreanCryptography/ as an extension of BerggrenLatticeReductionDuality. Define a parent function on PrimTriple using case splits over inverse Berggren matrices or word tails; prove parent_decreases_c, parent_primitive, iterate_parent_terminates, reduce_correct, reduce_normal_form_unique, and generation_reduction_inverse. Reuse well-founded recursion on Nat via hypotenuse c.\n\n\n### Catalog Context\n@FINAL/Geometry/BerggrenRamanujan.lean\n```lean\nimport Mathlib\n\n/-! # CatalogBuild.Pythagorean.Berggren.BerggrenRamanujan\n\nAuto-generated from theorem catalog database.\nDomain: Pythagorean/Berggren\nDeclarations: 59\n-/\n\nnoncomputable section\n\n/-- A direction in the ternary Berggren tree. -/\ninductive BDir where\n  | left  : BDir   -- B\u2081 branch\n  | mid   : BDir   -- B\u2082 branch\n  | right : BDir   -- B\u2083 branch\n  deriving DecidableEq, Repr, Inhabited\n\n/-- A position in the Berggren tree is a finite word over {left, mid, right}. -/\nabbrev BPos := List BDir\n\n/-- Apply a single Berggren step. -/\ndef berggrenStep (d : BDir) (t : \u2124 \u00d7 \u2124 \u00d7 \u2124) : \u2124 \u00d7 \u2124 \u00d7 \u2124 :=\n  let (a, b, c) := t\n  match d with\n  | .left  => (a - 2*b + 2*c, 2*a - b + 2*c, 2*a - 2*b + 3*c)\n  | .mid   => (a + 2*b + 2*c, 2*a + b + 2*c, 2*a + 2*b + 3*c)\n  | .right => (-a + 2*b + 2*c, -2*a + b + 2*c, -2*a + 2*b + 3*c)\n\n/-- The Pythagorean triple at a given position (path applied left-to-right from root). -/\ndef berggrenAt (path : BPos) : \u2124 \u00d7 \u2124 \u00d7 \u2124 :=\n  path.foldl (fun t d => berggrenStep d t) (3, 4, 5)\n\n/-- Each Berggren step preserves the Pythagorean equation. -/\ntheorem berggrenStep_preserves_pyth (d : BDir) (a b c : \u2124)\n    (h : a ^ 2 + b ^ 2 = c ^ 2) :\n    let (a', b', c') := berggrenStep d (a, b, c)\n    a' ^ 2 + b' ^ 2 = c' ^ 2 := by\n  cases d <;> simp [berggrenStep] <;> nlinarith [sq_nonneg (a - b), sq_nonneg (a + b)]\n\n/-- Every position in the Berggren tree yields a Pythagorean triple. -/\ntheorem berggrenAt_pyth (path : BPos) :\n    let (a, b, c) := berggrenAt path\n    a ^ 2 + b ^ 2 = c ^ 2 := by\n  simp only [berggrenAt]\n  suffices h : \u2200 (t : \u2124 \u00d7 \u2124 \u00d7 \u2124), t.1 ^ 2 + t.2.1 ^ 2 = t.2.2 ^ 2 \u2192\n    let r := path.foldl (fun t d => berggrenStep d t) t\n    r.1 ^ 2 + r.2.1 ^ 2 = r.2.2 ^ 2 from\n    h (3, 4, 5) (by norm_num)\n  intro t ht\n  induction path generalizing t with\n  | nil => exact ht\n  | cons d ds ih =>\n    simp only [List.foldl]\n    apply ih\n    exact berggrenStep_preserves_pyth d t.1 t.2.1 t.2.2 ht\n\n/-- Berggren matrix B\u2081. -/\ndef berggrenB\u2081 : Matrix (Fin 3) (Fin 3) \u2124 :=\n  !![1, -2, 2; 2, -1, 2; 2, -2, 3]\n-- ... (truncated, full file has 316 lines)\n```\n\n@FINAL/Geometry/RamanujanFrontiers.lean\n```lean\nimport Mathlib\n\n/-! # CatalogBuild.Pythagorean.ModularForms.RamanujanFrontiers\n\nAuto-generated from theorem catalog database.\nDomain: Pythagorean/ModularForms\nDeclarations: 79\n-/\n\nnoncomputable section\n\n/-- Berggren matrix B\u2081. -/\ndef rfB\u2081 : Matrix (Fin 3) (Fin 3) \u2124 :=\n  !![1, -2, 2; 2, -1, 2; 2, -2, 3]\n\n/-- Berggren matrix B\u2082. -/\ndef rfB\u2082 : Matrix (Fin 3) (Fin 3) \u2124 :=\n  !![1, 2, 2; 2, 1, 2; 2, 2, 3]\n\n/-- Berggren matrix B\u2083. -/\ndef rfB\u2083 : Matrix (Fin 3) (Fin 3) \u2124 :=\n  !![(-1), 2, 2; (-2), 1, 2; (-2), 2, 3]\n\n/-- The Lorentz form matrix: